WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:02.999
- Apparently someone at this
company thought I was charming

00:00:02.999 --> 00:00:05.598
and not like a total loser. (laughs)

00:00:05.598 --> 00:00:08.129
(playful music)

00:00:08.129 --> 00:00:09.793
- So, data science is a
pretty specialized field

00:00:09.793 --> 00:00:11.954
and I kind of fell into it by accident.

00:00:11.954 --> 00:00:14.055
I had no intentions of
being a data scientist.

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:19.055
- I moved to LA about a year
and six months ago with no job,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.253
no place to stay, none of that,

00:00:22.253 --> 00:00:24.836
and I was just kinda like
interning around the city of LA.

00:00:24.836 --> 00:00:26.480
- I had my own small YouTube channel

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.664
and I was a big BuzzFeed fan girl.

00:00:28.664 --> 00:00:30.615
I watched like all the videos.

00:00:30.615 --> 00:00:33.329
I like had all my favorites.

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:37.755
- I was in my final week
of college doing finals

00:00:37.755 --> 00:00:39.225
and I freaked out one night

00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:41.924
and was like well I
need to get a damn job.

00:00:41.924 --> 00:00:44.050
- I'm a very big soccer fan
and I was in a betting pool

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.203
with a bunch of my friends and
I was doing really well in it

00:00:46.203 --> 00:00:47.504
so I was like, you know what?

00:00:47.504 --> 00:00:49.415
I bet I could write an algorithm

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.948
that does all this betting for me on scale

00:00:51.948 --> 00:00:54.670
and I could just sit back
and just rake in the cash.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.328
So I went and starting
teaching myself data science

00:00:57.328 --> 00:01:00.241
using free online courses
for the sole purpose

00:01:00.241 --> 00:01:02.605
of writing a betting algorithm
and getting rich off of it.

00:01:02.605 --> 00:01:04.953
- When I found out that they were hiring,

00:01:04.953 --> 00:01:08.339
a friend of mine who had went
through the internship here

00:01:08.339 --> 00:01:09.802
said, "Oh, you'd be perfect for this.

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:11.271
"I'll recommend you."

00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:12.104
- One night I went out

00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:15.690
and I got extremely,
extremely intoxicated.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.103
I got back home at about
like three in the morning

00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:19.560
and my drunk friend convinced me

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.317
that I should look for jobs.

00:01:21.317 --> 00:01:24.076
- I applied for like
seven jobs at this company

00:01:24.076 --> 00:01:27.222
and then after that I
graduated, lived my life,

00:01:27.222 --> 00:01:30.938
and the night of my birthday
actually I got an email

00:01:30.938 --> 00:01:33.259
from Buzzfeed to do a video interview.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.972
So heres the thing, I was intoxicated

00:01:36.972 --> 00:01:39.923
because I had been drinking
since like 9:00 a.m.,

00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:44.288
and then I saw the interview
and I was like (bleep).

00:01:44.288 --> 00:01:47.760
I actually need to look
appropriate and look nice

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.332
and not look like I've been
having like five tequila shots.

00:01:51.332 --> 00:01:53.011
- I was like, you know,
BuzzFeed's a viral company.

00:01:53.011 --> 00:01:55.579
They like internet things
and trends on the internet.

00:01:55.579 --> 00:01:59.662
So, I tweeted: if this
tweet gets 1,000 retweets,

00:02:00.637 --> 00:02:01.974
BuzzFeed has to hire me

00:02:01.974 --> 00:02:03.806
because that's how the internet works.

00:02:03.806 --> 00:02:05.709
- I got called in for my interview.

00:02:05.709 --> 00:02:06.902
On the way I got very nervous

00:02:06.902 --> 00:02:08.258
so I kept checking my phone

00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:09.939
to make sure I was going the right way,

00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:11.938
and one of the times I
was checking my phone,

00:02:11.938 --> 00:02:13.887
don't check your phone while driving!

00:02:13.887 --> 00:02:17.957
I didn't notice the person
in front of me stopped moving

00:02:17.957 --> 00:02:19.508
and rolled right into them.

00:02:19.508 --> 00:02:21.384
I wasn't going super fast
but I definitely hit the car

00:02:21.384 --> 00:02:22.396
in front of me.

00:02:22.396 --> 00:02:23.229
- And I was like,

00:02:23.229 --> 00:02:24.224
oh, if this catches on it will be

00:02:24.224 --> 00:02:27.223
like the most epic BuzzFeed article.

00:02:27.223 --> 00:02:28.230
It didn't catch on.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.937
It got nowhere near 1,000 retweets.

00:02:29.937 --> 00:02:32.066
I don't even know if anyone
at BuzzFeed ever saw it.

00:02:32.066 --> 00:02:33.892
It was just kinda like a really sad thing

00:02:33.892 --> 00:02:35.208
and I was like I kinda
wanted to just delete

00:02:35.208 --> 00:02:36.109
the tweet just 'cause I was like,

00:02:36.109 --> 00:02:38.198
oh, this is embarrassing for
me that I put this out there

00:02:38.198 --> 00:02:40.354
and got nowhere near where I wanted.

00:02:40.354 --> 00:02:43.083
So I was like now it's just
a sad BuzzFeed article.

00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:45.502
- Immediately I called the
person I was meeting here

00:02:45.502 --> 00:02:47.642
at BuzzFeed for my
interview and being like,

00:02:47.642 --> 00:02:50.133
"I might be late, I just hit a car."

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:52.271
And I like kinda was starting to cry

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:55.009
and this person on the
other line was like,

00:02:55.009 --> 00:02:56.069
"Uh, are you okay?

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:58.235
"Don't worry about it we
can like adjust your time."

00:02:58.235 --> 00:03:00.408
I'm like, "No, no I think
I'll figure it out."

00:03:00.408 --> 00:03:02.990
- I did the video interview
and I looked at the camera

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.374
and I was like, "Hi, my name is Joyce

00:03:05.374 --> 00:03:06.861
"and I'm really fun-loving."

00:03:06.861 --> 00:03:09.856
I don't even remember
much of what it said.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:13.433
It was mostly like, "What's
your favorite BuzzFeed video?"

00:03:13.433 --> 00:03:14.526
And I couldn't think of crap.

00:03:14.526 --> 00:03:17.089
So I was like, "Oh, I
really love the dry ice."

00:03:17.089 --> 00:03:18.644
- So then the guy gets out of the car

00:03:18.644 --> 00:03:21.484
and we have a discussion and
he clearly just doesn't want

00:03:21.484 --> 00:03:24.202
to deal with me so he kinda, like, leaves.

00:03:24.202 --> 00:03:26.936
Never heard from this guy
again, so I'm still sorry sir.

00:03:26.936 --> 00:03:30.179
And I like get to BuzzFeed
and I'm still on time.

00:03:30.179 --> 00:03:32.020
I'm 5 minutes early.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.185
And they're like, "Do you need a moment?"

00:03:33.185 --> 00:03:34.403
And I'm like "No, no I got this."

00:03:34.403 --> 00:03:36.634
And I like ran upstairs still shaking.

00:03:36.634 --> 00:03:39.469
- Funny enough nobody from
BuzzFeed actually got back

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:42.718
to me when I applied on the
website and so I went online

00:03:42.718 --> 00:03:44.674
and went on Twitter and on LinkedIn

00:03:44.674 --> 00:03:46.826
and every recruiter I found
that was for Buzzfeed,

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:48.792
anybody that said anything
about a BuzzFeed type thing.

00:03:48.792 --> 00:03:50.335
I just started messaging them directly

00:03:50.335 --> 00:03:53.562
and after two months and I
think seven or eight stages

00:03:53.562 --> 00:03:55.170
of interviews I eventually got hired

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.364
to be the first data scientist
in BuzzFeed LA's operations.

00:03:58.364 --> 00:04:00.324
- While I was out here
interning I ended up being

00:04:00.324 --> 00:04:02.839
an extra in a BuzzFeed video

00:04:02.839 --> 00:04:05.922
where I met another
production coordinator here.

00:04:05.922 --> 00:04:07.285
We ended up getting coffee,

00:04:07.285 --> 00:04:09.374
and then I just kind of told
him some of my experiences

00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:11.772
and then I showed him the tweet.

00:04:11.772 --> 00:04:13.178
He laughed at it and he was like,

00:04:13.178 --> 00:04:15.018
"Yeah no, that's not gonna work."

00:04:15.018 --> 00:04:16.680
But he told me to apply for the position.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.054
I applied, interviewed,
and the rest is history.

00:04:20.054 --> 00:04:21.608
- And at the end of the
interview I was like,

00:04:21.608 --> 00:04:23.915
"Oh, what are you looking
for in an employee here?"

00:04:23.915 --> 00:04:24.748
And they said, you know,

00:04:24.748 --> 00:04:27.050
someone who kinda goes with the flow.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.360
And I was like, "Well, if
you want someone that goes

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.053
"with the flow, I got in
an accident on the way here

00:04:32.053 --> 00:04:34.324
"and still made it to
my interview on time."

00:04:34.324 --> 00:04:35.904
And they were like...

00:04:35.904 --> 00:04:38.334
- And then like a couple days
later I was asked to come

00:04:38.334 --> 00:04:40.665
to California for the real interview.

00:04:40.665 --> 00:04:42.217
And they really liked me

00:04:42.217 --> 00:04:45.466
and it's all because I got
drunk one night on my birthday.

00:04:45.466 --> 00:04:47.883
- Overall I would say the
funniest part of all of this is,

00:04:47.883 --> 00:04:51.353
you know, me landing this
really big career jump

00:04:51.353 --> 00:04:56.353
as a result of gambling and
drinking that got me here.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:57.500
- I made it!

00:04:58.383 --> 00:05:00.168
Fan girl turned employee.

00:05:00.168 --> 00:05:01.967
It's a Cinderella story.

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:04.964
- If you put it out there, I
mean, whatever your goal is,

00:05:04.964 --> 00:05:05.915
whatever your dreams are,

00:05:05.915 --> 00:05:08.574
no matter how like ridiculous
they may seem at the time.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:11.533
It's really weird that in some way,

00:05:11.533 --> 00:05:13.963
I think putting it out
there makes it more real.

00:05:13.963 --> 00:05:15.016
So you know, put it out there.

00:05:15.016 --> 00:05:18.417
The universe might give it to you.

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:21.084
(playful music)

